1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,025
You already know what to do.

2
00:00:03,405 --> 00:00:06,430
That's the part nobody wants to look at.

3
00:00:06,810 --> 00:00:11,135
The plan isn't missing. The information
isn't missing.

4
00:00:11,515 --> 00:00:15,940
You could write the steps down right now,
in order, from memory.

5
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:18,670
And still, nothing moves.

6
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:24,775
So you tell yourself the truth as you
understand it. You're just not feeling it
yet.

7
00:00:25,875 --> 00:00:31,225
That sentence sounds like an observation.
It isn't. It's a strategy.

8
00:00:31,605 --> 00:00:36,055
Underneath it sits a rule you never agreed
to out loud.

9
00:00:36,435 --> 00:00:42,885
Action requires a feeling first. Get the
feeling, then act. No feeling, no action.

10
00:00:43,265 --> 00:00:46,715
It's a clean rule. It's also backwards.

11
00:00:47,095 --> 00:00:50,920
And it gets more expensive every time you
apply it.

12
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:56,470
Here's the strange part. You already have
evidence against it.

13
00:00:56,850 --> 00:01:01,675
Think about the last time you did
something hard without wanting to.

14
00:01:02,055 --> 00:01:04,530
You got out of bed for a flight.

15
00:01:04,910 --> 00:01:07,310
You showed up for someone else.

16
00:01:07,690 --> 00:01:10,840
You answered a message you'd been
dreading.

17
00:01:11,220 --> 00:01:16,760
No motivation arrived first. You moved,
and the resistance thinned out somewhere
in

18
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:17,795
the middle.

19
00:01:18,175 --> 00:01:23,800
You have run that experiment hundreds of
times. It keeps coming out the same way.

20
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:26,855
So why does the rule survive?

21
00:01:27,955 --> 00:01:31,005
Because waiting pays. Immediately.

22
00:01:31,385 --> 00:01:36,635
Procrastination research points at
something less flattering than laziness.

23
00:01:37,015 --> 00:01:43,320
Fuschia Sirois and Timothy Pychyl argued
in two thousand thirteen that delay

24
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:45,665
functions as short-term mood repair.

25
00:01:46,045 --> 00:01:51,320
The task carries discomfort. Putting it
off removes the discomfort now.

26
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:56,600
The cost gets handed to a version of you
who isn't in the room yet.

27
00:01:56,980 --> 00:02:01,840
So you're not failing at discipline.
You're succeeding at something else.
Feeling

28
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:04,230
better in the next ten minutes.

29
00:02:05,330 --> 00:02:07,530
And the bill compounds.

30
00:02:07,910 --> 00:02:13,240
Every time you skip a task while telling
yourself you need more motivation, you

31
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:16,510
teach yourself one more thing about that
task.

32
00:02:16,890 --> 00:02:20,515
That it requires an unusual state to
attempt.

33
00:02:20,895 --> 00:02:24,745
Ordinary tasks get promoted into heavy
ones.

34
00:02:25,125 --> 00:02:31,100
A folder of unpaid bills becomes a
confrontation. A thirty minute walk
becomes an

35
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:32,750
identity test.

36
00:02:33,130 --> 00:02:37,255
Nothing about the work changed. The price
tag did.

37
00:02:37,635 --> 00:02:42,660
That's the trap, and it's circular.
Waiting for motivation is the thing that
makes

38
00:02:42,660 --> 00:02:45,060
the task need motivation.

39
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:48,260
Now the useful part.

40
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:53,280
If motivation follows action, you'd expect
that to show up somewhere serious.

41
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:57,390
Somewhere with higher stakes than a tidy
desk.

42
00:02:57,770 --> 00:03:02,460
It does. There's a treatment for
depression built on exactly that order of

43
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:06,495
operations. It's called behavioral
activation.

44
00:03:06,875 --> 00:03:11,880
Patients schedule activity before the mood
that would support it exists. Not when

45
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:14,550
they feel ready. On a schedule.

46
00:03:14,930 --> 00:03:24,660
In a two thousand six randomized trial led
by Sona Dimidjian, with two hundred forty

47
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:29,160
one adults, behavioral activation
performed comparably to antidepressant
medication

48
00:03:29,161 --> 00:03:30,705
among the more severely depressed
patients.

49
00:03:31,085 --> 00:03:36,580
I want to be careful here. A later
reanalysis did not reproduce its advantage
over

50
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:43,210
cognitive therapy, so treat this as one
strong result, not a settled ranking.

51
00:03:43,590 --> 00:03:49,020
But the order of operations held up well
enough to become standard practice. You
can

52
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:54,165
act your way into the state. You cannot
reliably wait your way into it.

53
00:03:54,545 --> 00:03:59,680
If that order works in the treatment of
clinical depression, your Tuesday
afternoon

54
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:02,045
is not a special case.

55
00:04:03,145 --> 00:04:05,495
Which changes the question.

56
00:04:05,875 --> 00:04:11,040
The question was never how to feel more
like it. The question is how much it costs

57
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:12,250
you to start.

58
00:04:12,630 --> 00:04:17,330
And cost is a design problem, not a
character problem.

59
00:04:17,710 --> 00:04:23,020
Peter Gollwitzer's work on implementation
intentions is the cleanest version of

60
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:24,010
this.

61
00:04:24,390 --> 00:04:30,215
Instead of writing a goal, you write a
trigger. If this situation, then this
action.

62
00:04:30,595 --> 00:04:36,800
Not exercise more. Instead, when I finish
dinner on Monday, I put on my shoes and

63
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:38,820
walk to the end of the street.

64
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:46,560
A two thousand six meta analysis by
Gollwitzer and Sheeran, covering ninety
four

65
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:51,580
independent tests, found a medium to large
effect on goal attainment. A d of point

66
00:04:51,824 --> 00:04:52,525
six five.

67
00:04:52,905 --> 00:04:58,355
Notice what the if then plan removes. Not
the discomfort. The decision.

68
00:04:58,735 --> 00:05:03,920
Deciding is the expensive part. And it's
exactly the part you were asking
motivation

69
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:05,735
to pay for.

70
00:05:06,835 --> 00:05:09,135
Here's where I stop selling.

71
00:05:09,515 --> 00:05:16,200
In two thousand ten, Phillippa Lally's
team tracked ninety six people adopting a

72
00:05:16,201 --> 00:05:17,965
daily behaviour for eighty four days.

73
00:05:18,345 --> 00:05:23,895
The number that escaped into the internet
is sixty six days to form a habit.

74
00:05:24,275 --> 00:05:30,740
The fuller version is less tidy. Sixty six
days is the median among the people whose

75
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:32,050
data fit the curve.

76
00:05:32,430 --> 00:05:39,180
Estimates ran from about eighteen days to
a projection past two hundred fifty. And

77
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:43,880
roughly half the participants never
crossed the threshold inside the study
window.

78
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:50,460
So arrangement beats motivation. It is
still not fast, and it is not guaranteed.

79
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:56,700
One more correction worth having. For
years the popular story was willpower as a

80
00:05:56,700 --> 00:05:59,390
fuel tank that drains through the day.

81
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:07,760
In two thousand sixteen, a twenty three
lab preregistered replication with two

82
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:14,380
thousand one hundred forty one
participants put that effect near zero. A
d of point

83
00:06:14,381 --> 00:06:15,770
zero four, with a confidence interval
crossing zero.

84
00:06:16,150 --> 00:06:21,440
That doesn't prove the effect is absent.
It does mean you shouldn't build your week

85
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:23,450
on the fuel tank metaphor.

86
00:06:23,830 --> 00:06:29,000
Which is quietly good news. If willpower
isn't a tank, you aren't waiting for it to

87
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:30,230
refill.

88
00:06:31,330 --> 00:06:35,705
None of this is new. It's just been said
less carefully since.

89
00:06:36,085 --> 00:06:41,600
Aristotle, in the Nicomachean Ethics, puts
virtue in the category of habit rather

90
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:43,135
than feeling.

91
00:06:43,515 --> 00:06:49,540
In the Ross translation, we become just by
doing just acts, and brave by doing brave

92
00:06:50,027 --> 00:06:50,415
acts.

93
00:06:50,795 --> 00:06:56,920
William James, in eighteen ninety, wrote
that we should make automatic and
habitual,

94
00:06:56,920 --> 00:07:01,470
as early as possible, as many useful
actions as we can.

95
00:07:01,850 --> 00:07:07,700
Both are saying the same unglamorous
thing. The state you want is downstream.

96
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:12,900
So the instruction is small, and a little
disappointing.

97
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:18,505
Pick the one task you've been circling.
Don't make it meaningful. Make it cheap.

98
00:07:18,885 --> 00:07:24,580
Write one sentence in the form, when X
happens, I do Y. Make Y small enough that
it

99
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:26,185
doesn't need a state.

100
00:07:26,565 --> 00:07:31,115
Then do it once while still unconvinced.
That part matters.

101
00:07:31,495 --> 00:07:36,220
Because the motivation you were waiting
for was never the ticket.

102
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:39,775
It's the receipt. It shows up after.

103
00:07:40,155 --> 00:07:45,160
If you write that one sentence, put it in
the comments as the if then. Not the goal,

104
00:07:45,420 --> 00:07:47,705
the trigger. I read those.
